// Practice quiz: Debugging a failing request
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/validation-errors-and-testing/debugging-a-failing-request

::o1 reading trace::In a stack trace, which frame is usually the first useful place to look? {
~The very last frame#Not quite. That is usually thread startup.
~Any frame in Tomcat#Not quite. That is usually infrastructure.
=The topmost frame in your own package#Correct. That is where your code hit the failure.
~The first line of the log file#Not quite. That may be from startup.
}

::o2 reproduce::Why write a failing test before fixing a bug? {
~It makes the code faster#Not quite. That is not the purpose.
~Tests cannot be written after fixes#Not quite. They can.
~It avoids reading logs#Not quite. You still read the logs.
=It proves the bug and then proves the fix#Correct. It also stops regressions.
}

::o3 4xx::A 400 appears with no stack trace in your code. What is likely? {
=Binding or validation rejected the input#Correct. The controller edge stopped it.
~A null reference in the service#Not quite. That would be a 5xx with a trace.
~A database crash#Not quite. That would be a 5xx.
~A JVM crash#Not quite. That would show no response at all.
}

::o3 5xx::A 500 has a Hibernate exception at a repository call. Which layer owns the failure? {
~The client#Not quite. A 5xx is a server problem.
=The repository or data access layer#Correct. The frame and exception point there.
~The HTTP method#Not quite. It would give 405.
~The JSON format#Not quite. That would give 400.
}

::o3 405::What does 405 tell you about a failing request? {
=The path exists, but that HTTP method is not mapped#Correct. Check the method the client sends.
~The server is down#Not quite. Then nothing would respond.
~Validation failed#Not quite. That is usually 400.
~The resource was deleted#Not quite. That is usually 404.
}

